I'm going to lead off talking about Cairn by stating a few general facts:
1. I climb a lot. It's one of my favorite hobbies, and one I've done for almost a decade
at this point. Specifically I boulder, I fucking hate heights. So naturally I played this
game where you lead climb a giant mountain.
2. Cairn's climbing simulation is seriously impressive. You can definitely get Aava to do
some...uh...interesting yoga moves if you really try, but on the whole it's legitimately
pretty bang on accurate to how I myself would have done a certain sequence of moves (not
that I'm the best climber, I'm startlingly average).
3. This game managed to give me a combination of some of the most satisfying and rewarding
moments I can look back on fondly while also presenting maybe some of the most frustrating
experiences I've had in years with a video game. I yelled at my TV alone in my apartment a
couple times. The section with the spire can eat so, so many dicks. But, just like in real
climbing, you are absolutely rewarded once you perservere.

Cool, now that all the boring shit about *checks notes* the actual video game is out of
the way, lets talk about the narrative. Because I really did not expect it to hit as hard
as it did. It punches way, way above its weight way more than it has any right to (or
maybe I'm just at a point in my life where it spoke to me on some higher level).

At its core it's not a very complicated nor even super unique tale, at least broad strokes
wise. You play as Aava, a world-famous climber who's now reaching the end of her prime.
You've decided that you want to climb Mount Kami, which is functionally a fictionalized
version of Everest (it's not explicitly said that it's the tallest mountain in the world,
but it is described as the hardest climb in the world that no one has ever summited).
Along the way you encounter Marco, a much younger climber who's looked up to you since he
was a kid, and serves as a foil to Aava's relentless pursuit of the summit at the cost of
everything else in her life. You're also presented with messages from friends and loved
ones along the way, in the form of audio notes that your belay-bot plays for you during
the climb. Aava makes it a point to never respond to any of these, even those from her
wife, even though they have an obvious emotional effect on her (especially towards the
end).

You also spend much of the game climbing through the homes, temples, and townships carved
into the mountain by the people known as Troglodytes (which, side note, was perhaps a
blemish on an otherwise tight narrative for me. Troglodytes? Really? We couldn't have
thought of a less on-the-nose name for them?). They've recently-ish abandoned their
homeland to descend the mountain and live amongst wider society as it's begun to encroach
further and further onto their way of life. The game makes a special point to call out
their interactions with thrill-seeking climbers as well, scattering various notes from
Troglodyte folks who figuratively shake their heads at those who see the mountain as
nothing more than a checkbox on their list.

Aava, too, sets out with this mindset, though she can't see it in herself. She dispairages
those that came before her while at the same time describing Kami as her greatest
challenge, something she will push towards and conquer, all that sort of terminology. And
in most ways, that doesn't change for the majority of the game. Both literally and
figuratively, it is her final mountain to conquer. No one can sway her away from it, not
Marco, not Damas Al Fatri (a veteran climber who went missing 12 years ago and never
finished his ascent, choosing to stop just below where everyone else has died), not the
messages from her wife, not her ever-faithful climbot finally giving out. Sure, the game
presents you with the option to end the climb early, but I actually wish it hadn't. It
isn't Aava's character. She's meant to give every single ounce towards this one totally
singular goal, and that's what she does.

Something changes at the end though. The game has a bit of an ambiguous ending when Aava
does finally reach that summit. After pushing past where anyone has gone before, nearly
succumbing to multiple avalanches, and losing her climbot, she falls to her knees on the summit
beneath the stars. The game then has this sort of magical sequence where Aava begins to
climb into the sky, using the stars as handholds and footholds. After a few moments of
this, it simply says "Soar," and Aava launches herself into the constellations, where the
credits role. The literal implication is clearly that Aava conquers the mountain with her
last ounce of strength, only to die on the summit, and this is her mind's final moments.
Something about how it's presented though, especially with how excellent both the music
and visuals are, really spoke to me. The way it's done, I read it as Aava finally
coming to terms with the fact that the climb meant more than summit. It's a tried and true
trope of course (journey before destination yadda yadda), but I really liked the way Cairn
presents it, in a bit of an open-ended interpretation. As she flies into the sky above the
peak of Kami, Aava has both succeeded in her ultimate goal and given up on everything that
made it meaningful in the first place. Depressing, but real. And real in the human sense.

Maybe it was just because of my own life circumstance right now, but Cairn really hit me
harder than I expected. It's short, it's heartfelt, and it is most definitely art. I leave
you with this screenshot I took of a pretty fantastic vista spot pretty early in the game.
[Damn.]($image.asset('vista.jpg').size(800,0))
